Title: VLM2Vec: Training Vision-Language Models for Massive Multimodal Embedding Tasks

Abstract: Embedding models have been crucial in enabling various downstream tasks such as semantic similarity, information retrieval, and clustering. Recently, there has been a surge of interest in developing universal text embedding models that can generalize across tasks (e.g., MTEB). However, progress in learning universal multimodal embedding models has been relatively slow despite their importance. In this work, we aim to explore the potential for building universal embeddings capable of handling a wide range of downstream tasks. Our contributions are twofold: (1) MMEB (Massive Multimodal Embedding Benchmark), which covers 4 meta-tasks (i.e. classification, visual question answering, multimodal retrieval, and visual grounding) and 36 datasets, including 20 training and 16 evaluation datasets, and (2) VLM2Vec (Vision-Language Model -> Vector), a contrastive training framework that converts any state-of-the-art vision-language model into an embedding model via training on MMEB. Unlike previous models such as CLIP and BLIP, VLM2Vec can process any combination of images and text to generate a fixed-dimensional vector based on task instructions. We build a series of VLM2Vec models on Phi-3.5-V and evaluate them on MMEB's evaluation split. Our results show that VLM2Vec achieves an absolute average improvement of 10% to 20% over existing multimodal embedding models on both in-distribution and out-of-distribution datasets in MMEB.

Introduction:
Embeddings, or distributed representations, encode inputs (whether text or images) as fixed-dimensional vectors, enabling a range of downstream tasks. These tasks include semantic similarity, information retrieval, clustering, classification, and question answering, among others. In recent years, there has been a surge of interest in developing universal text embedding models that can generalize across tasks \cite{muennighoff2022mteb}. However, progress in learning universal multimodal embedding models has been relatively slow despite their importance. Unlike text embedding models, multimodal embedding models must handle inputs with different modalities, such as text and images, and are more complex to train and evaluate. This complexity arises from the need to align and integrate information from multiple modalities, which is not as straightforward as in text-only models. Additionally, the scarcity of multimodal datasets and the diversity of tasks involving multiple modalities make it challenging to develop a unified evaluation framework. In this work, we aim to explore the potential for building universal embeddings capable of handling a wide range of downstream tasks. Our contributions are twofold:

\begin{itemize}
\item MMEB (Massive Multimodal Embedding Benchmark), which covers 4 meta-tasks (i.e. classification, visual question answering, multimodal retrieval, and visual grounding) and 36 datasets, including 20 training and 16 evaluation datasets. MMEB includes 8.7 million training samples and 690K evaluation samples. To the best of our knowledge, MMEB is the largest multimodal embedding benchmark to date, surpassing the previous largest benchmark by 4x in training samples and 3x in evaluation samples.
\item VLM2Vec (Vision-Language Model -> Vector), a contrastive training framework that converts any state-of-the-art vision-language model into an embedding model via training on MMEB. Unlike previous models such as CLIP \cite{radford2021learning} polysemy can cause problems. For example, the class name ``bird'' may refer to an animal, a type of aircraft, or a device for holding a cup of coffee. For each dataset we experimented with 10 different prompt engineering strategies to address this issue. For each dataset we experimented with 10 different prompt engineering strategies to address this issue. We found that prompt engineering is important for zero-shot transfer and that ensembling multiple strategies can further improve performance. We provide an overview of our prompt engineering strategies in Appendix \ref{sec:prompt-engineering}.} and BLIP, which can process any combination of images and text to generate a fixed-dimensional vector based on task instructions. Unlike previous models, VLM2Vec can process any combination of images and text to generate a fixed-dimensional vector based on task instructions. We build a series of VLM2Vec models on Phi-3.5-V and evaluate them on MMEB's evaluation split. Our results show that VLM2Vec achieves an absolute average improvement of 10\% to 20\% over existing multimodal embedding models on both in-distribution and out-of-distribution datasets in MMEB.
