Related Work
\paragraph{Multimodal Embedding Benchmarks}
There have been several efforts to create multimodal benchmarks for evaluating embedding models. Some of the most prominent ones include MMEB \cite{jiang2024vlm2vec}, MMEB2 , and MMEB3. These benchmarks evaluate models on tasks such as classification, visual question answering, and multimodal retrieval, covering a wide range of datasets. However, they have limitations such as limited size and lack of task diversity. For instance, MMEB1 and MMEB2 have only 12 and 16 datasets respectively, and MMEB3 has 10. In contrast, our MMEB benchmark includes 36 datasets, covering a broader range of tasks and providing a more comprehensive evaluation framework for multimodal embedding models.

\paragraph{Multimodal Embedding Models}
There have been several efforts to develop multimodal embedding models that can handle a wide range of downstream tasks. Some of the most prominent ones include CLIP \cite{zhu2023clip}, BLIP, and ViT-CLIP . These models are trained on large-scale multimodal datasets and can handle different modalities such as text and images. However, they are not designed to be universal and may not generalize well to new tasks. In contrast, our \model framework is designed to be universal and can handle a wide range of downstream tasks. It can be trained on any combination of images and text and can be adapted to new tasks with minimal effort.

\paragraph{Multimodal Large Language Models}
There have been several efforts to develop multimodal large language models (MLLMs) that can handle a wide range of downstream tasks. Some of the most prominent ones include LLaVA \cite{liu2023visual}, MiniGPT-4 \cite{zhu2023minigpt-4}, and InstructBLIP. These models are trained on large-scale multimodal datasets and can handle different modalities such as text and images. However, they are not designed to be universal and may not generalize well to new tasks. In contrast, our \model framework is designed to be universal and can handle a wide range of downstream tasks. It can be trained on any combination of images and text and can be adapted to new tasks with minimal effort. <|section_end|>

